<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abkhaz people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Abkhazians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abkhaz are a people of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> who live mainly in Abkhazia, on the eastern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. They speak Abkhaz, a Northwest Caucasian language. Ruled by their own princes under Georgian and later Ottoman influence, they came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the 19th century, and many were deported or emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the 1860s and 1870s. Abkhazia broke away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in the war of 1992 and 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people.jpg" alt="Abkhaz people" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apsuwara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abkhaz-deputatklk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
